WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.125
ResearchBoost creates source-linked presentations that connect a field's
problem, statistics, history, frontier, and next experiments.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:15.550
It selects work for challenging problems, usable ideas, strong baselines,
research-group contributions, and demonstrated scaling.

00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:18.350
Here is an example of entering a neighboring field.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.200
My background is multi-agent coordination for LLM agents.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.000
To understand robot teams, I give Codex related papers and invoke
ResearchBoost.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.825
It expands the literature, maps the field, and explains the evidence.

00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:38.500
The following deck illustrates the output, connecting familiar agent ideas
with robotics constraints and evaluations.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.950
The title defines the scope: agent coordination, with robot teams as the
focal case.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.350
That boundary tells newcomers which questions the field map will answer.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.175
The agenda shows the route from the research landscape to evidence and
open questions.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.750
It gives readers a structure for organizing unfamiliar concepts.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.950
The basket lift makes a dependency concrete: both grips must be ready.

00:01:03.025 --> 00:01:07.575
This failure explains why team coordination requires more than capable
individuals.

00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:12.950
The normalized trend chart shows research activity within defined venues.

00:01:13.025 --> 00:01:17.325
Its denominators help separate topic visibility from changes in source
coverage.

00:01:18.375 --> 00:01:24.950
The annual views advance from 2022 to 2026, showing which questions
attract attention.

00:01:25.025 --> 00:01:29.100
It reveals movement within the field, beyond a single overall growth
curve.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.500
The period totals compare the size of research directions in the coded
subset.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:38.775
They help prioritize reading while keeping annual fluctuations in
perspective.

00:01:39.825 --> 00:01:43.950
The application comparison shows where coordination methods are evaluated.

00:01:44.025 --> 00:01:46.525
It clarifies well-tested settings and gaps.

00:01:47.575 --> 00:01:54.275
The domain comparison exposes robotics-specific constraints: space,
contact, timing, and safe recovery.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.850
It explains why familiar ideas from software agents need different
physical tests.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:03.475
The matrix connects research directions with downstream tasks.

00:02:03.525 --> 00:02:09.300
It helps locate the robotics literature within the broader field and spot
gaps between methods and applications.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.750
This slide separates robot tasks from evaluation settings.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:20.825
Distinguishing simulation, physical execution, and hardware data prevents
treating different kinds of evidence as equally strong.

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:26.925
The specialist-venue statistics place coordination beside neighboring
robotics topics.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.850
This shows how the focal area fits into robotics, alongside general agent
research.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:38.150
The application map distinguishes protecting fleet flow from enabling a
joint physical task.

00:02:38.225 --> 00:02:42.125
It connects abstract coordination mechanisms to practical outcomes.

00:02:43.175 --> 00:02:46.575
The warehouse examples show how shared routes cause blocking.

00:02:46.625 --> 00:02:52.600
They establish why coordination quality should be measured through
operational outcomes, not planning elegance alone.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.100
The handoff example connects task delegation with the timing of physical
actions.

00:02:58.175 --> 00:03:03.100
It shows why allocating roles does not, by itself, ensure successful
cooperation.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:08.525
The community map links groups and open projects to concrete
contributions.

00:03:08.575 --> 00:03:13.275
It helps newcomers find useful sources and follow the communities shaping
subsequent work.

00:03:14.325 --> 00:03:19.250
The architecture comparison identifies who can select or revise a shared
action.

00:03:19.325 --> 00:03:24.250
This distinction is essential for fair comparisons of centralized and
local approaches.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.650
This slide explains the changing constraints behind new coordination
questions.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.125
Understanding those drivers makes a causal history of the field.

00:03:34.175 --> 00:03:38.025
The timeline links milestones to the questions they leave unresolved.

00:03:38.075 --> 00:03:41.175
It helps readers understand the foundations of current methods.

00:03:42.225 --> 00:03:46.700
Classical routing establishes a known-map planning baseline and its
assumptions.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:51.050
That reference makes it easier to see which constraints later systems
actually relax.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:56.400
The CERBERUS case brings unreliable maps and communication into the story.

00:03:56.475 --> 00:04:00.625
It reveals which assumptions become fragile outside controlled warehouses.

00:04:01.675 --> 00:04:05.575
RoCo connects language-based joint plans with motion checks.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:10.100
This shows why a plausible language plan still needs grounding in physical
feasibility.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.075
PARTNR exposes task dependencies and coordination failures.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:21.450
A benchmark perspective helps readers identify missing capabilities
instead of comparing isolated demonstrations.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.500
The selection slide makes the frontier criteria explicit before comparing
recent works.

00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:33.325
It gives viewers a consistent basis for judging importance, evidence, and
scale across the examples.

00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:40.350
The frontier map organizes representative works by problem, authority,
mechanism, and scale.

00:04:40.425 --> 00:04:45.525
It provides an overview for the deep dives and prevents treating unlike
approaches as interchangeable.

00:04:46.575 --> 00:04:51.900
RoboBallet illustrates how task assignment and motion planning become one
mechanism.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:56.550
Understanding that intervention makes its contribution more concrete than
its headline result.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.125
The training slide explains the policy inputs, outputs, and learning
process.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.925
This reveals the data and optimization assumptions behind its coordination
capability.

00:05:07.975 --> 00:05:12.475
The result slide compares trajectory cost under specified baseline
conditions.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.850
Reading the trade-off alongside the method shows what improvement is
actually supported.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.600
CHORUS separates shared training from local decisions at runtime.

00:05:21.675 --> 00:05:26.325
This distinction prevents confusing a common model with centralized
control of the team.

00:05:27.375 --> 00:05:32.875
The action model and loss explain how coordinated demonstrations become
local policies.

00:05:32.925 --> 00:05:36.975
They connect the training design to the behavior the method is expected to
produce.

00:05:38.025 --> 00:05:41.950
The handover ablation isolates shared versus separate policy weights.

00:05:42.025 --> 00:05:47.325
Such a controlled comparison helps explain why a method works, rather than
only whether it succeeds.

00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:52.925
The three-robot task makes the tested team scale and training conditions
visible.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.350
This keeps a successful demonstration from becoming a claim of general
scalability.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.675
RoboTalk combines messages and tool calls within the learned behavior.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:07.725
It explains how communication becomes part of coordination when a
teammate's state is hidden.

00:06:08.775 --> 00:06:13.350
The results distinguish held-out task performance from a matched rationale
ablation.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:18.875
Keeping those comparisons separate prevents overstating what the evidence
says about generalization.

00:06:19.925 --> 00:06:25.325
HOLA introduces unfamiliar partners, layouts, and team sizes as distinct
changes.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.450
This clarifies which form of adaptability a coordination method is being
asked to demonstrate.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.400
The training algorithm shows how the population of partners changes.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:40.225
It connects exposure during learning with the teammate diversity tested at
evaluation.

00:06:41.275 --> 00:06:46.200
The generalization results separate new partners from harder combinations
of changes.

00:06:46.275 --> 00:06:51.375
This shows why robustness must be specified by test setting, rather than
claimed universally.

00:06:52.425 --> 00:06:56.775
The first future slide connects observed limitations to open questions.

00:06:56.825 --> 00:07:01.800
It helps a newcomer identify research opportunities grounded in the
field's actual evidence.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:07.945
The next slide turns those questions into datasets, methods, baselines,
and metrics.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:11.670
This makes the field map useful for choosing a concrete next experiment.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.845
The conclusion returns to the basket and separates local responses from
shared decisions.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.845
It turns the survey into a usable principle for reasoning about new
coordination problems.

00:07:23.895 --> 00:07:28.945
The paper list groups readings by coordination problem and explains why
each is useful.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.570
It gives the viewer a practical route from this overview into deeper
study.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.995
Bring quality papers, reviews, notes, and research-group links.

00:07:37.045 --> 00:07:41.225
Add your background, question, audience, and talk length, then invoke
research boost.

00:07:41.275 --> 00:07:43.700
Use the result to decide what to read and test next.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:51.275
Thank you to Pascale Fung for the field-history inspiration, and Graham
Neubig for the research-impact inspiration through their EMNLP twenty
twenty-six keynotes.

00:07:51.325 --> 00:07:55.100
However you use AI to speed up research, someone is moving faster.

00:07:55.175 --> 00:07:57.975
Build your next research advantage with ResearchBoost.
